  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve as the percentage of the US' hen flock that was sexed with in-ovo technology according to official Innovate Animal Ag reporting as of Q1 2027.",
  "fine_print": "If Q1 2027 reporting is not released before July 2027, or If Innovate Animal Ag significantly changes its criteria such that previous reporting is not comparable, this question can be resolved by another credible source if it is comparable. Otherwise, the question will be **annulled**.",
  "background": "According to [Innovate Animal Ag](https://innovateanimalag.org/about), a non-profit, non-partisan think tank,\n\n> [In-ovo sexing](https://innovateanimalag.org/egg-sexing) is an innovative hatchery technology where the sex of a chicken egg can be determined while it is still developing. This allows egg producers to hatch only females, providing an alternative to the culling of day-old male chicks.&#x20;\n> &#x20;\n> The technology has advanced rapidly over the last few years, is widely available in Europe and entered the US market in late 2024. In-ovo sexing companies are racing to scale up the technology to meet this increase in global demand.\n\nMarket penetration and forecast [research](https://innovateanimalag.org/market-penetration-forecast) in Q1 2025 found:\n\n> In-ovo sexing is just beginning its rollout in the US, and currently represents less than 1% of the US’ 310 million head commercial layer flock.\n\n> The global penetration of in-ovo sexing is expected to continue its sharp growth in the next few years, led by rapid expansion in the US....",
